Natural causes, like changes in the Sun or volcanic eruptions, can't explain the warming. The Sun's output has barely changed in decades. Carbon from burning [[@oil-coal|coal and oil]] has a slightly different chemical fingerprint from other carbon, and scientists can see that fingerprint growing in the air.

Bubbles of ancient air trapped in Antarctic ice show that carbon dioxide is now higher than at any time in at least 800,000 years. The Intergovernmental Panel on Climate Change, which brings together thousands of scientists from around the world, says it is clear that human activity has warmed the planet.

{{story:solar-panels}}

Yellowstone sits on top of one of the world's largest volcanic systems. Its last huge eruption happened roughly 630,000 years ago. Scientists watch it closely every day.

In 1872, the USA made Yellowstone the world's first [[@national-parks|national park]], protecting its geysers, forests and wildlife for everyone. Nearby, Steamboat Geyser is the tallest active geyser in the world, sometimes shooting water over 90 m high, though it can go years between big eruptions.

{{story:yellowstone-wolves}}

In 1915, Einstein added gravity to the picture. Close to a heavy object, time runs slower. Higher up, where gravity is a little weaker, time runs faster.

This means your head is [[@ageing|ageing]] very slightly faster than your feet. In 2010, scientists in the United States used two extremely accurate clocks to show that raising one by just 33 cm made it run measurably faster. Near a [[@black-holes|black hole]], where gravity is extreme, the effect would be enormous.

{{story:einstein}}

In the 1990s, Japan's fast Shinkansen [[@bullet-trains|bullet trains]] had a problem. When they shot into tunnels at high speed, they pushed a wave of air ahead of them that burst out of the far end with a loud boom. Engineer Eiji Nakatsu, a keen birdwatcher, thought of how a kingfisher dives into water with barely a ripple.

His team gave the 500 Series train a long, pointed nose shaped like a kingfisher's beak. When it entered service in 1997, it was quieter in tunnels and used less electricity, and it ran faster. It is one of the best-known examples of engineers copying nature to solve problems, an approach called biomimicry.

{{story:bullet-trains}}

[[@ancient-egypt|Ancient Egypt]] loved cats. They appear in paintings, statues and jewellery. Cats protected granaries and killed snakes, and they were linked to Bastet, a goddess of the home, often shown with a cat's head.

Egyptians mummified huge numbers of cats as offerings to Bastet. So many were buried that in the late 1800s, a ship carried about 19 tonnes of cat mummies from Egypt to England, where most were ground up to spread on fields. A few survived in museums.

{{story:mummies}}

A mosquito looking for blood picks up the carbon dioxide in your breath from several metres away. It flies upwind, zigzagging through the trail. As it gets closer, it senses your warmth and the moisture coming off your skin. At the last moment, your skin's smell helps it decide where to land.

That smell comes partly from [[@sweat|sweat]] and partly from the [[@microbes-friendly|bacteria that live on your skin]], which turn sweat into smelly chemicals. Feet and ankles have lots of bacteria, which may be why some mosquitoes love to bite there.

{{story:mosquito-diseases}}

If a sailor points the boat too close to the wind, the sail flaps uselessly. Sailors call this area the no-go zone. To reach a place directly upwind, they sail at an angle, first to the left and then to the right, turning the front of the boat through the wind each time.

Each turn is called a tack. A boat beating upwind traces a zigzag across the water, like someone climbing a steep hill by a winding path. It travels further, but it gets there.

{{story:airplanes-fly}}

A wick is a braid of cotton threads with tiny gaps between them. Liquids creep up narrow gaps like these on their own. Scientists call it [[capillary|capillary]] action, and it is the same effect that pulls water up the fine tubes in a plant.

The wick has to be the right size. Too thin, and it can't lift enough wax, so the flame shrinks and dies. Too thick, and it lifts more than the flame can burn, which makes a smoky, flickering candle. Many modern wicks are braided so they curl over as they burn. The tip pokes into the hottest part of the flame and burns away, so nobody has to trim it.

{{story:space-station}}

The Gotthard Base Tunnel took 17 years to build. Four giant tunnel boring machines, each about as long as four football fields, chewed through the Alps. They dug out about 28 million tonnes of rock. That is enough to build five copies of the Great Pyramid of Giza.

At its deepest, the tunnel lies about 2.3 km below the peaks. That deep, the [[@earth-layers-crust|Earth's heat]] pushed rock temperatures to about 45°C. Fast trains now cross the Alps in about 20 minutes inside the mountain, instead of climbing over it.

{{story:earth-layers-crust}}

For about 2,000 years, nearly all the world's diamonds came from India. They were washed out of riverbeds along the Krishna River and traded at [[@golconda|Golconda]], near today's Hyderabad. The [[@kohinoor|Koh-i-Noor]] and the famous blue Hope Diamond, now in the Smithsonian in Washington DC, both came from Indian mines.

In the 1720s, diamonds were found in Brazil. Then in 1867, a child in South Africa picked up a shiny stone near a riverbank, which turned out to be a big diamond. Within a few years, huge mines opened there. The biggest gem diamond ever found, the Cullinan, came from a South African mine in 1905. It weighed 3,106 carats, about 620 grams, as heavy as a small papaya.

{{story:space-weird-diamonds}}

De Mestral had been out hunting in the Swiss countryside with his dog. Afterwards, both of them were covered in burrs, the spiky seed cases of the burdock plant. Instead of just picking them off, he looked at one under a [[@microscopes|microscope]].

He saw that each spike ended in a tiny hook. The hooks caught on anything with loops, such as the threads in his trousers and the curly hair of his dog. Plants use this trick to spread their [[@seeds-fly|seeds]], which hitch rides on passing animals and fall off somewhere new.

{{story:seeds-fly}}

The story begins with the shining blue-tiled mosques and palaces of Persia and Central Asia, where craftsmen perfected blue glazes over many centuries. The technique of making glazed tiles and pots travelled into India with Turkic and [[@mughals|Mughal]] rulers. You can see similar blue tiles on old buildings in Lahore, Delhi and elsewhere in north India.

Jaipur's own tradition grew in the 1800s. Sawai Ram Singh II, who ruled Jaipur from 1835 to 1880, loved the craft and sent local artisans to learn it. He set up a school of art in the city that helped the craft grow.

{{story:mughals}}

Take a square grid of dots and try to draw one line that weaves around every dot and ends where it started. This kind of kolam, called a *sikku* or *kambi* kolam, is a real puzzle. Experienced artists draw them quickly from memory, sometimes with dozens of dots.

[[@maths-art|Mathematicians describe these patterns]] using symmetry and graph theory, the maths of points and the lines joining them. Many kolams match themselves four times in one full turn, like a plus sign. Others have mirror lines running across them. Some grow outward in repeated loops that look a little like [[@fractals|fractals]], patterns built from smaller copies of themselves.

{{story:honeycomb}}

In the 1940s, milk from Kaira was sent to the city of Bombay. A private company held the contract to collect it and paid the farmers very little. Middlemen took much of the rest.

The farmers asked for advice from [[@sardar-patel|Sardar Vallabhbhai Patel]], who came from the same area. He told them to form their own [[@cooperatives|cooperative]]. Local leader Tribhuvandas Patel helped organise them. In 1946, the farmers refused to sell milk to the company for about two weeks, and the government agreed to buy from their new union instead. It was registered on 14 December 1946.

{{story:sardar-patel}}

Rajendra Singh trained in ayurvedic medicine and worked for the government. In 1985, he and a few friends quit their jobs and went to live in a village in Alwar, planning to run health and education work.

An old farmer there told him the village didn't need more schooling. It needed water. He told Singh to dig. So Singh began clearing out an old johad, carrying mud in baskets. When the monsoon came, the pond filled, and nearby wells that had been dry for years started to hold water.

{{story:rainwater-harvesting}}

In November 1992, in a Test match in Durban, Sachin Tendulkar became the first batter given out by a third umpire watching TV replays. Since then, technology has grown fast. Cricket added a full review system with ball-tracking around 2008 and 2009. Tennis began using Hawk-Eye at big tournaments in 2006, and in 2025 [[@tennis-grand-slams|Wimbledon]] replaced its line judges with electronic calling.

{{story:computer-vision}}

Football introduced video assistant referees, or VAR, at the 2018 men's World Cup. In 2022, Stéphanie Frappart of France became the first woman to referee a men's World Cup match. Cameras catch things eyes can miss, but people still have to decide what a replay means, and arguments about [[@fairness|fairness]] haven't gone away. Players, fans and officials still argue about handballs and offside lines measured in centimetres, much as they did before any camera arrived.

Each Super Bowl is numbered with [[@roman-numerals|Roman numerals]]. Super Bowl I was in 1967. Super Bowl LIX, which means 59, was played in 2025. Because the season starts in one year and ends in the next, Roman numerals avoided confusion over which year's champion it was. They also make the game sound grand.

The winners receive the Vince Lombardi Trophy, a sterling silver football on a stand, named after the coach who won the first two Super Bowls. Each winning player also gets a large ring.

{{story:advertising}}

Before writing code, app makers try to understand the people who will use their app. What do they need? What confuses them? A product manager often decides what the app should do first and what can wait.

Designers then draw the screens, first as rough boxes called wireframes, then as detailed pictures. Good [[@design-everyday|design]] makes an app feel obvious: buttons where your thumb expects them, words that are clear, colours that guide your eye. Designers often test paper or clickable prototypes with real people and change them many times.

{{story:design-everyday}}

Under the sea there are no windows to look out of and light fades within a few hundred metres. Submarines "see" with sound. Sonar works much like the [[@echolocation|echolocation]] bats and dolphins use: send out a ping, listen for the echo, and work out how far away something is.

Most of the time, though, military subs listen silently, so as not to give away where they are. Near the surface, a [[@periscopes|periscope]] or a mast with cameras pokes up to look around.

{{story:echolocation}}

Carthage made its money from the sea. It traded tin and silver from Spain, grain from African farms and a costly purple dye made from sea snails. Its people spoke Punic, a form of the Phoenician language, and wrote with the Phoenician [[@alphabet-history|alphabet]]. A Carthaginian captain named Hanno is said to have sailed far down the west coast of Africa, around 500 BCE.

The city had two linked harbours. The outer one was for merchant ships. The inner one was round, with an island in the middle. Around its edges and on the island stood sheds for well over a hundred warships, and from a tower in the centre the admiral could see them all. You can still see the outline of that round harbour beside the sea today.

{{story:sea-ports}}

On 18 May 1974, under Prime Minister Indira Gandhi, scientists led by Raja Ramanna set off a device in a shaft at Pokhran. India called it a "peaceful nuclear explosion". The code name was Smiling Buddha, because the day was Buddha Purnima.

Other countries were not convinced it was peaceful. Canada and the US cut back nuclear help, and a group of countries formed new rules to control the sale of nuclear materials. For the next 24 years India did not test again, though work continued in secret.

{{story:cold-war}}

Alaska is so far north that the Sun behaves strangely. In Utqiaġvik, the [[@extreme-northernmost-town|northernmost town]] in the USA, the Sun does not set for about 80 days in summer. In winter, it stays below the horizon for about two months. People use blackout curtains in summer and bright lamps in winter.

The long nights have a bonus. Alaska is one of the best places on Earth to see the [[@aurora|aurora]], the [[@solar-wind|northern lights]], glowing green and purple across the sky.

{{story:aurora}}

Happiness sounds impossible to count. Bhutan's answer is a long questionnaire. Trained [[@surveys|surveyors]] visit thousands of households and ask people about nine areas of life, including psychological wellbeing, health, time use, good governance and how connected they feel to their community.

The answers are combined into a GNH Index. It shows which groups are doing well and which are struggling, so that money can be aimed where it is most needed. Other countries, and even the United Nations, have borrowed parts of the idea when they measure wellbeing.

{{story:surveys}}

During the [[@world-war-two|Second World War]], American bombers destroyed large parts of many Japanese cities. Kyoto was largely spared. In 1945, it was on an early list of targets for the atomic bomb. Henry Stimson, the US Secretary of War, had visited Kyoto as a tourist years earlier. He argued that destroying it would be a terrible loss and had it removed from the list.

Because of this, Kyoto still has a great many buildings that are hundreds of years old, more than almost any other big Japanese city.

{{story:video-game-history}}

In the 1960s, India worried about famine. Scientists brought in new, high-yielding wheat and rice, along with fertilisers and wells. [[@people-farmers-day|Punjab's farmers]] took them up quickly, and harvests jumped. This became known as the [[@green-revolution|Green Revolution]].

Punjab covers only about 1.5% of India's land, but for decades it has supplied a big share of the grain the government buys for ration shops. That success has a cost. Pumping so much groundwater for rice has made water tables fall in many districts. And when farmers burn leftover straw after the rice harvest, the smoke adds to the [[@air-pollution|air pollution]] that hangs over northern India each autumn.

{{story:bhangra}}

Scientists sort the world's plants into six "floral kingdoms". Most are enormous, covering whole continents. One of them, the Cape Floral Kingdom, fits into a small corner of South Africa.

It holds around 9,000 kinds of plants, and about 70 percent are [[endemic|endemic]], found nowhere else. Table Mountain alone has more plant species than the whole of Britain. The most famous is the king protea, a flower as big as a dinner plate. Many of these plants need fire to spread, and their seeds sprout only after a wild blaze has swept through.

{{story:biomes}}
